WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.570
Lia is crossing the city while talking to Davi.

2
00:00:04.570 --> 00:00:09.420
Along the way, one tower's signal weakens and
another grows stronger.

3
00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:15.160
In this proposed mobility architecture, the
device itself guides the change of access.

4
00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:21.420
Before leaving the first tower, the device
prepares access through the second.

5
00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:24.880
A stronger signal is a reason to try.

6
00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:30.070
Authorization and an available path allow it to
start using that access.

7
00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:36.550
During the transition, PRP can carry the same
data over two authorized paths.

8
00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:42.370
The redundancy is deliberate: one copy can
arrive even when the other encounters a

9
00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:44.040
failure along the way.

10
00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:49.780
At the destination, the copies are recognized as
the same data.

11
00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:54.880
The duplicate is discarded before becoming a
second delivery to the application.

12
00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:59.980
The paths change, but the communication remains
bound to the same participants.

13
00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:07.360
When the new path is ready to support the
communication, the device leaves the previous
one.

14
00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:12.870
In this architecture without a mobile core, the
handover decision belongs to the client.

15
00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:14.840
The relationship with Davi remains.

16
00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:18.780
Using two paths consumes more resources.

17
00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:22.630
Redundancy can therefore be limited to the
transition.

18
00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:28.400
Its protection depends on alternatives that
remain available when one path fails.

19
00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:33.730
Prepare the next path before leaving the
previous one.

20
00:01:33.730 --> 00:01:37.520
Mobility with continuity built around the
relationship. PRP.

